  "preamble": "The shift from the voiceless velar stop [k] to a glottal stop [ʔ] is a form of debuccalization. In many English dialects, velar and dental stops in coda positions lose their oral articulation, leaving only the glottal closure. Xavánte (Central Jê, Brazil) turned every Proto-Akuwẽ *k into ʔ, as in ʔa 'white' against Xerente ka.",
